From: renner.dev
Subject: wsi/win32: present on the DXGI_SHARED path from a thread

vkQueuePresentKHR on DXGI_SHARED waited on the CPU for the image's WSI fence, then copied the image into the DXGI
swapchain through D3D11 and presented, all on the application's thread: about 1.9 ms per frame on top of the GPU
time, never overlapped with the next frame (Windows 11 guest, RX 9070 XT host, renner-bench at 53% of native).

The present thread does that work now: vkQueuePresentKHR queues the image (in order, at most 16) and returns; the
thread waits, copies, presents and marks the image idle. A queued image is not acquirable until it was presented,
so the image count still bounds how far the application gets ahead. A failure reaches the application through
chain->status at the next present or acquire. Destroying the swapchain presents what is queued, then joins the
thread. If the thread cannot start, presents stay inline as before.

renner-bench, 1280x720, immediate, idle host GPU, 3 rounds alternating: native 4277/4288/4261; installed Venera
0.2.3 2262/2306/2041; the same Mesa built without this patch 2232/2189/2220; with it 3612/3738/3469 (about 84% of
native).

